Internalized Ableism / Stigma Distress - Adjustment Disorder - V62.89 (Target of perceived adverse discrimination)
Diversity Equity and Inclusion
Limitation concealment, Productivity-linked worthlessness, Overexertion symptom exacerbation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DEI-023
Reason for Visit: Acute physical collapse and severe exacerbation of primary disability symptoms directly resulting from intentional, extreme physical overexertion, requiring urgent stabilization.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and roommate
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute, severe post-exertional crash with the patient completely unable to mobilize, accompanied by intense physical pain and muscle fasciculations. Urgent out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was mandated to rule out rhabdomyolysis, severe electrolyte derangement, or an acute neurological event prior to the availability of routine medical services.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I pushed myself to work exactly like everyone else today to prove I'm not broken, and now my muscles are twitching uncontrollably and I can't stand up.
History of Present Illness: The patient describes a chronic history of denial and active concealment of physical limitations to appear 'normal' in their workplace. Over the past 48 hours, driven by intense self-criticism regarding the need for reasonable accommodations, the patient pushed their physical limits far past established safety thresholds to meet able-bodied standards. This resulted in an acute, severe exacerbation of their primary symptoms (overexertion symptom exacerbation). The patient notes profound feelings of worthlessness linked to their current low productivity, chronic grief stemming from the inability to meet internalized ableist expectations, and apologizing excessively for the inconvenience caused by the collapse. The roommate notes a deeply ingrained belief that the patient's value is directly tied to their economic output.
Functional Status: Acutely functionally impaired; bedbound, unable to independently transfer to a chair or toilet due to profound weakness and pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of recurrent severe physical crashes and long-term functional decline due to persistent, unsafe limitation concealment.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 105 | Temp: 37.1 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Patient is lying flat in bed, visibly exhausted, tearful, and apologizing repeatedly to the clinician for the callout.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Mild reactive tachycardia, normal heart sounds. Respiratory: Clear bilaterally. Musculoskeletal: Diffuse, severe muscle tenderness across bilateral lower extremities. Visible, widespread muscle fasciculations (twitching) in the quadriceps and calves. Gross motor strength is globally reduced to 3/5 in the lower limbs, secondary to profound fatigue and pain, not a primary focal neurological deficit. Neurological: Cranial nerves intact. Sensation intact. The exam highlights severe limitation concealment; the patient attempts to sit up unsupported during the exam, immediately failing due to weakness, driven by productivity-linked worthlessness.
Environmental Observations: Mobility aids (a cane and joint braces) are hidden in the back of the closet, not readily accessible.
Medication Reconciliation: No changes to baseline disability medications, but the patient delayed their afternoon dose today while attempting to "push through" the work shift.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Internalized Ableism / Stigma Distress - Adjustment Disorder - V62.89 (Target of perceived adverse discrimination) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Rhabdomyolysis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of dark, tea-colored urine, normal temperature, and the specific known mechanism of overexertion within the context of their chronic illness, though close monitoring is required.

Acute Stroke / TIA - Ruled out/unlikely due to the bilateral, symmetrical nature of the weakness, the presence of diffuse muscle pain and fasciculations, and intact cranial nerves.

Major Depressive Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary cause, as the profound feelings of worthlessness are exclusively tied to productivity and physical capability (internalized ableism), rather than generalized anhedonia.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe physical collapse, reactive tachycardia (HR 105), and widespread muscle fasciculations resulting directly from profound physical overexertion driven by internalized stigma. Immediate out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was critical to conduct a thorough neurological and musculoskeletal exam to definitively exclude an acute stroke or impending severe rhabdomyolysis. Delaying care until routine hours would have left the patient bedbound, unable to access fluids or a toilet safely, and at high risk for a severe fall or metabolic decompensation, definitively justifying urgent medical intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Initiated strict bed rest and oral fluid replacement to flush muscle breakdown products. Prescribed a short course of oral diazepam (2mg as needed) to manage severe muscle fasciculations. Advised the usual GP to arrange psychometric profiling showing high levels of internalized self-stigma and review renal function (U&E) and CK levels.
Positive Behavioral Change: To combat limitation concealment safely, implement a "visible aid protocol": keep the mobility cane physically visible next to the front door at all times; normalizing the visual presence of the aid in the home reduces the psychological barrier to using it when needed. To manage overexertion symptom exacerbation, utilize a "pacing timer": set a strict 30-minute timer for all physical tasks, followed by mandatory 10 minutes of complete rest; logging adherence to this boundary rewards safe energy management over raw productivity.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a disability-competent occupational therapist and specialized psychological counseling.
DME/Safety (Optional): Immediately moved mobility aids from the closet to the bedside.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP review within 48 hours to assess physical recovery, review blood tests, and coordinate long-term disability support.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or contact 000 if experiencing completely dark/tea-colored urine, sudden inability to breathe deeply, or severe, unremitting localized muscle pain.

